WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.939 --> 00:00:05.250
Science has long studied how animals talk
to one another.

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:09.850
They may not have language like you and I
but almost every animal on the planet has

00:00:09.850 --> 00:00:15.190
some form of communication system, sometimes
these can be simple, like the bark of a dog

00:00:15.190 --> 00:00:19.680
or extremely complex like the sonar waves
of a Dolphin.

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:24.790
Seriously dolphins are better communicators
than most humans, scientists have found that

00:00:24.790 --> 00:00:30.820
dolphins can actually send a complete image
via sonar, so if Terry the dolphin likes the

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:36.300
look of a certain fish he can actually send
an accurate photo of that fish to his mate

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:40.629
Steve the dolphin, so Steve can come and help
Terry to catch it.

00:00:40.629 --> 00:00:46.079
This is the biological equivalent of humans
sending each other holograms, which we can’t

00:00:46.079 --> 00:00:47.269
even do yet.

00:00:47.269 --> 00:00:52.559
So basically dolphins are the omnipotent master
race and we have unknowingly been living on

00:00:52.559 --> 00:00:55.070
their planet for millions of years.

00:00:55.070 --> 00:01:00.629
But that’s enough about our evil dolphin
overlords, what about other fishy communication

00:01:00.629 --> 00:01:01.629
systems?

00:01:01.629 --> 00:01:04.610
How about the humble herring?

00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:11.640
Well herring are quite simply the undisputed
Nobel Prize winners of underwater communication,

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:15.510
as they use farts to talk to each other.

00:01:15.510 --> 00:01:20.980
Speaking of Nobel Prizes, the fellow who discovered
this, Dr Bob Batty at the Scottish Association

00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:26.420
for Marine Science, won the Ig Nobel Prize,
an award for research that first makes people

00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:30.820
laugh and then makes people think, for his
fish fart research.

00:01:30.820 --> 00:01:36.170
Dr Batty and two of his colleagues in Canada
became enthralled by herring farts when they

00:01:36.170 --> 00:01:41.670
noticed that small gas bubbles are rapidly
released from the rear of herrings every night.

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:46.760
So obviously they immediately dropped all
their actual research and setup round-the-clock

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:51.680
infrared video cameras and underwater microphones
to monitor herrings.

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:58.070
They noticed that every night, when the sun
goes down the herring start to break wind

00:01:58.070 --> 00:02:03.200
and they picked up very high-pitched raspberry-like
noises on the microphones.

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:07.270
It has long been common knowledge that herring
swim to the surface to take in huge amounts

00:02:07.270 --> 00:02:12.030
of air then store it in their bladder and
then when they see a predator they release

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:16.530
one huge bubble of air from their bottom to
attempt to scare off the predator.

00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:22.450
But through Bob Batty and his Canadian colleagues’
research, we now know that they do a very

00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:26.810
similar thing to talk to the rest of their
shoal.

00:02:26.810 --> 00:02:31.800
What the team noticed was that the more herring
that are in the tank, the more often they

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:36.600
fart, indicating that it must be some form
of communication.

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:41.920
The main way the herrings use their behind
to communicate is to maintain order within

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:43.010
the shoal.

00:02:43.010 --> 00:02:49.030
When herring school they do so in a perfect
grid pattern with immaculately measured spacing

00:02:49.030 --> 00:02:50.740
in-between one-another.

00:02:50.740 --> 00:02:56.040
The gap left between each fish is the typical
distance that a prey will jump away from the

00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:58.430
herring when it attempts to eat it.

00:02:58.430 --> 00:03:03.130
Therefore, this grid formation allows for
optimal schooling.

00:03:03.130 --> 00:03:08.080
So that’s all very well but how does a school
of thousands of herring perfectly organize

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:11.599
themselves into an immaculate grid formation?

00:03:11.599 --> 00:03:14.630
By farting in each other’s faces.

00:03:14.630 --> 00:03:19.460
By releasing noxious gas from their bottom
into the face of the fish behind them, they

00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:25.300
can precisely control the distance between
each other and maintain their perfectly-spaced

00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:26.300
grid.

00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:30.610
It’s kind of like constantly farting on
the train so nobody sits next to you.

00:03:30.610 --> 00:03:35.930
But these aren’t your average farts, herring
rapidly release around 60 short bursts of

00:03:35.930 --> 00:03:40.459
gas from their anal duct, over approximately
6 seconds.

00:03:40.459 --> 00:03:46.380
The resulting sound is a single, continuous
burst of high-frequency noise.

00:03:46.380 --> 00:03:50.489
Scientists have named these FRTs, I’m not
kidding.

00:03:50.489 --> 00:03:54.160
Which stands for, Fast Repetitive Tick sounds.

00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:59.450
The benefit of this noise is that most other
fish can’t hear this high-pitched buzzing

00:03:59.450 --> 00:04:02.080
sound, but herring can.

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:07.940
So by only farting to communicate they are
able to travel in huge schools and remain

00:04:07.940 --> 00:04:11.120
relatively undetected by predators.

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:16.359
In 1994 Sweden almost went to war with Russia
over herring farts.

00:04:16.359 --> 00:04:21.930
The Swedish military detected an almost metallic
clicking sound underwater, incredibly similar

00:04:21.930 --> 00:04:24.900
to the noise produced by Russian submarines.

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:29.790
Sweden went on high alert and dispatched military
units to search for intruders within their

00:04:29.790 --> 00:04:30.790
waters.

00:04:30.790 --> 00:04:35.790
Sweden even wrote a letter to Russia demanding
them to withdraw their submarines from their

00:04:35.790 --> 00:04:37.380
waters at once.

00:04:37.380 --> 00:04:42.810
But they quickly swept the whole thing under
the rug when a Swedish scientist discovered

00:04:42.810 --> 00:04:48.970
that the sound they recorded was not enemy
submarines but a school of thousands of farting

00:04:48.970 --> 00:04:50.530
herrings.

00:04:50.530 --> 00:04:55.820
Nature is weird and sometimes it seems like
evolution has totally lost it.

00:04:55.820 --> 00:05:01.330
It’s not just herring though, human’s
do some pretty strange stuff too, but no matter

00:05:01.330 --> 00:05:06.740
how weird you think you are, just remember,
at least you don’t talk out of your arse,

00:05:06.740 --> 00:05:26.370
unless your name is Donald Trump.

